A 1B dense-equivalent MoE design (16 experts, top-k=2 → ~1.07B active, ~6.7B total) in Megatron-DeepSpeed format:

```json
{
  "framework": "megatron-deepspeed",
  "architecture": {
    "num_experts": 16,
    "top_k": 2,
    "hidden_size": 2048,
    "num_layers": 16,
    "capacity_factor": 1.25,
    "expert_intermediate_size": 6144,
    "seq_len": 2048,
    "total_params_b": 6.7,
    "active_params_b": 1.07,
    "dropout": 0.0,
    "dtype": "bf16"
  },
  "parallelism": {
    "data_parallel": 2,
    "expert_parallel": 4,
    "tensor_parallel": 2,
    "total_gpus": 16,
    "micro_batch_size": 4,
    "gradient_accumulation_steps": 16,
    "global_batch_tokens": 262144
  },
  "batch_size": 262144,
  "estimated_memory_gb": 48,
  "estimated_tokens_per_sec": 35000,
  "launch_notes": "Per-GPU ~48GB on 16xH100-80GB (bf16 weights ~13GB replicated base + EP-sharded experts, Adam fp32+moments ~80GB sharded across EP, activations with seq-parallel + activation checkpointing). All-to-all dispatch cost is small since only ~2/16 of tokens per layer are routed. Launch with DeepSpeed: deepspeed pretrain_gpt.py --deepspeed_config ds_config.json; ds_config sets moe.enabled=true, moe.num_experts=16, moe.top_k=2, moe.capacity_factor=1.25, moe.ep_size=4, zero_optimization.stage=1, bf16 enabled, activation-checkpointing on. Capacity 40,960 tokens/expert/layer >> 32,768 expected routed, so token dropping is negligible and load is balanced. Tokenizer: GPT2ByteLevel. Scheduler: cosine decay, warmup 2%, batch ramp 128->262k tokens. Expected throughput ~35k tok/s => 262k-token step every ~7.5s."
}
```

Design rationale: attention (~268M) + 2/16 of expert FFN active per token gives ~1.07B active (dense-equivalent); EP=4 shards the 16 experts across ranks while TP=2 keeps expert all-to-all small; capacity 1.25 avoids token dropping at k=2.